<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    structsynth locals: a pointer a call returned, read or written at constant
    offsets, is declared a pointer to a record of its own.

    Source (gcc -O2 -fcf-protection=none -fno-pic, xalloc/lookup/consume
    left outside the image so nothing declares what they return):

        struct node { struct node *next; long key; int count; char tag; };
        extern void *xalloc(unsigned long);
        extern struct node *lookup(long key);
        extern void consume(char *);

        struct node *make(long key, struct node *next)        // @0x100000
        { struct node *n = xalloc(sizeof *n);
          n->next = next; n->key = key; n->count = 1; n->tag = 'n';
          return n; }

        long weigh(long key)                                   // @0x100030
        { struct node *n = lookup(key);
          return n->key + n->count + (long)n->next; }

        void label(void)                                       // @0x100050
        { char *p = xalloc(16);
          __builtin_memcpy(p, "SCHILY.xattr.", 14); consume(p); }

    With option structsynth param (asserts 1 and 2): only parameters are
    measured, so every access through the returned pointer is raw offset
    arithmetic, *(char *)((int8)v1 + 0x14) = 0x6e and (int8)(int4)v1[2].

    With option structsynth locals (asserts 3 to 6): the returned value is the
    local's only definition, so its accesses are measured like a parameter's
    and it is declared struct_0 * / struct_1 *, the reads and writes naming
    field_0x0, field_0x8, field_0x10 and field_0x14.

    label's buffer is filled with the bytes of a string literal, and a buffer
    of text is not a record: it keeps its pointer type in both passes
    (assert 7).
-->
